### 6\. How to connect all five levels in security research

Consider the example of an Android application receiving a username from a server.

### One piece of data, five representation levels

1\. Meaning

Username: `ADMIN`

2\. Data structure and format

JSON object containing a username field

3\. Encoding

UTF-8 character encoding

4\. Binary representation

`01000001 01000100 01001101 01001001 01001110`

5\. Physical implementation

Electrical states in memory and electrical or radio signals during transmission

### 7\. What to investigate as an offensive security researcher

These are particularly valuable areas to investigate:

| Area                    | Security questions                                          |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Data structures         | Can incorrect structure handling cause memory corruption?   |
| File formats            | Can malformed headers confuse a parser?                     |
| Encodings               | Can inconsistent decoding cause validation bypass?          |
| Serialization           | Can untrusted objects trigger unsafe deserialization?       |
| Binary representation   | Can integer overflow or truncation occur?                   |
| Memory layout           | Can incorrect bounds handling cause out-of-bounds access?   |
| Physical implementation | Can hardware or side-channel weaknesses expose information? |
| Network representation  | Can malformed packets trigger parser failures?              |
| Executable formats      | Can malformed executable metadata cause unsafe processing?  |

My recommendation: Master sections 2, 3 and 4 before moving deeply into section 5. Those three will give you the foundation for file-format analysis, protocol analysis, reverse engineering, binary exploitation research and fuzzing.

No finite list can contain literally every representation ever developed, but these categories cover the major established families and many important specialized ones.
